Request real periodic sync frames on the HEVC MediaCodec encoder path.

0002-libavcodec-mediacodecenc-Only-flag-real-IRAP-NALs-as-key-frames.patch
stopped the container lying about which packets are real seek points, but
left the actual cause untouched: on the Galaxy S24 Ultra (Qualcomm
c2.qti.hevc.encoder, SM8650), the encoder still only ever emitted one true
IDR NAL per encode, no matter what "i-frame-interval"/gop_size asked for.
Every seek still decoded from t=0 — fast for a short clip, not for a long
one.

What is confirmed: "i-frame-interval" reaches the encoder as the Codec2
scalar parameter coding.sync-frame-interval (seen live in this device's
own CCodecConfig dump, set to the expected value), and does produce a
periodic intra picture — but `trace_headers` on a real compression shows
that picture is coded as an ordinary TRAIL_R NAL (type 1), not IDR (type
19/20): 25 "key" packets, 1 real IDR, 24 TRAIL_R. The device's own config
dump also lists a second, vector-typed parameter, coding.gop, logged as
an "ignored struct field" during FFmpeg's generic AMediaFormat configure
— that one plausibly controls the actual per-frame type assignment
(I/P/B, real IDR vs. plain intra) and FFmpeg has no way to set it, but
that specific mechanism is a hypothesis, not confirmed; what IS confirmed
is the TRAIL_R-not-IDR result itself.

The same config dump separately lists "coding.request-sync-frame.value"
as a live, scalar, per-frame-request parameter. That is the same thing
Android's public MediaCodec API exposes as PARAMETER_KEY_REQUEST_SYNC_FRAME
/ AMEDIACODEC_KEY_REQUEST_SYNC_FRAME (the string "request-sync") via
MediaCodec.setParameters(Bundle) / AMediaCodec_setParameters(AMediaFormat)
— a request made while the codec is running, not a configure-time
option, with a documented contract: the codec signals a real sync frame
at or shortly after the next frame submitted. Whether issuing it actually
promotes the resulting NAL to IDR on this specific encoder is the thing
this patch sets out to test; see "Unverified" below.

This patch:
 1. Adds setParameters support to both backends (mediacodec_wrapper.c/.h):
    JNI via a reflected android.os.Bundle (setParameters takes a Bundle in
    the Java API, not a MediaFormat/AMediaFormat, hence the separate
    Bundle class/constructor/putInt handles), NDK by dlsym'ing
    AMediaCodec_setParameters (available since API 26) the same way
    setInputSurface and signalEndOfInputStream already are. Both expose
    one new wrapper call, requestSyncFrame(), that builds the one-key
    "request-sync" parameter set and sends it; either backend returns
    AVERROR(ENOSYS) when the underlying call isn't available, which the
    caller treats as "stop trying", not fatal.
 2. In mediacodecenc.c, HEVC-only (avctx->codec_id == AV_CODEC_ID_HEVC):
    remembers avctx->gop_size as a frame count (mce_sync_gop_frames,
    distinct from the seconds-based "i-frame-interval" already sent to
    the codec), counts frames sent in mediacodec_send(), and calls
    requestSyncFrame() right before queuing the frame that completes one
    GOP. The counter resets whether or not the call succeeds, so a
    transient failure is retried at the next GOP boundary rather than on
    every following frame (the first draft of this patch reset it only
    on success, which logged a failure on every single frame once the
    call started failing). A result of ENOSYS disables the feature for
    the rest of this encode (mce_sync_gop_frames = 0) instead of retrying
    forever. The first success and the first failure are each logged
    once at AV_LOG_INFO — the level the app's own diagnostics capture —
    so a field log can distinguish "never ran", "ran but failed" and
    "ran" without needing debug verbosity; the wrapper's own per-call
    log is AV_LOG_DEBUG, to avoid a second, noisier line on every retry.
    H.264 is untouched: the bug was only observed on this hardware HEVC
    encoder, and this changes runtime behavior of every encode on
    whichever codec it's applied to, not just packet flagging like 0002.

Unverified: whether requestSyncFrame() actually makes c2.qti.hevc.encoder
promote that frame to a real IDR NAL (type 19/20) rather than the TRAIL_R
it currently uses. This is the standard, documented Android mechanism for
forcing a sync frame, and the device's own config dump shows the
matching Codec2 parameter live, but it has not been run on an S24 Ultra.
If it works, 0002 becomes unnecessary for new encodes (every "key"
packet will be a real IRAP) but stays correct and harmless to keep,
including for files already encoded without this fix. If
requestSyncFrame() comes back ENOSYS or the device rejects the
parameter, or the resulting NALs are still TRAIL_R, this patch does
nothing beyond what 0002 already does, and must be reported as such
rather than assumed to work — the INFO log lines above exist specifically
to tell that apart from the actual ffprobe/trace_headers result.

Verification needed on-device (S24 Ultra, H.265 to MKV/MOV, a clip at
least a minute or two long — a 25 s clip is too short for 0002 alone to
look any different from a full fix): re-encode, then run both of:
  ffprobe -v error -select_streams v:0 -show_entries packet=flags \
    -of csv=p=0 OUT.mkv | sort | uniq -c
  ffmpeg -hide_banner -i OUT.mkv -map 0:v -c copy -bsf:v trace_headers \
    -f null - 2>&1 | grep nal_unit_type | awk '{print $NF}' | sort -n | uniq -c
A fixed encoder shows a NAL type 19 or 20 at every GOP boundary, not just
the first; before this patch only the very first one is type 19 and the
rest are type 1 (TRAIL_R). Cross-check against the app's own diagnostics
log for the "Issue 104 workaround" INFO lines this patch adds.

